Git Branching Notes (Interview + Practical Understanding)
1. What is a Branch in Git?

A branch is an independent line of development in Git. It allows developers to work on new features, bug fixes, or experiments without affecting the main code.

Simple Definition:

A branch is like a copy of your project where you can safely make changes.

Real-world Use Case

Your banking application is running in production.

main → Stable production code
feature/login-mfa → Develop Multi-Factor Authentication
bugfix/payment-error → Fix payment issue
release/v2.0 → Prepare next release

Each developer works on their own branch and later merges it into main after testing.

2. Why do we use branches instead of committing everything to main?
Reasons

✅ Keeps main stable

✅ Multiple developers can work simultaneously

✅ Easy testing before release

✅ Easy rollback if something goes wrong

✅ Supports CI/CD pipelines

Example

Suppose 5 developers are working.

Developer	Branch
Dev A	feature-login
Dev B	feature-payment
Dev C	bugfix-dashboard
Dev D	feature-report
Dev E	feature-api

If everyone commits directly to main:

Code conflicts increase
Production may break
Difficult to identify bugs

Using branches keeps work isolated until it's ready.

3. What is HEAD in Git?

HEAD is a pointer that tells Git which branch and latest commit you are currently working on.

Think of it as:

"You are here" in your Git repository.

Example:

main

A ---- B ---- C
              ↑
            HEAD

If you switch to another branch:

feature-login

A ---- B ---- D
              ↑
            HEAD

HEAD always points to your current working branch (or commit).

Interview Answer

HEAD is a reference to the currently checked-out branch or commit. Git uses HEAD to know where new commits should be added.

4. What happens to your files when you switch branches?

When you change branches:

Git updates your working directory.
Files become exactly as they were in that branch.
Files added only in another branch disappear (until you switch back).
Modified files may prevent switching if changes are not committed or stashed.
Example

main

app.java
README.md

Switch to feature-login

app.java
README.md
login.java
login.css

Git automatically changes your files to match the selected branch.

